patch: fail a hunk whose first line is invalid.
When a line inside a hunk does not start with ' ', '+', '-' or '\',
patch calls fail_hunk() and goes on to look for the next file. But
fail_hunk() returned at once if the hunk had no lines yet, so a hunk
whose very first line was invalid failed silently: no "Hunk FAILED"
message, exit status 0, and since patch was no longer in a hunk, the
remaining hunks for that file were skipped while the hunks before it
were still written out. The file was left half patched with no sign
of trouble.

Let fail_hunk() handle an empty hunk, so that it reports the failure,
sets the exit status and discards the changes to the file, as it does
for any other failed hunk.

---
 editors/patch.c |   11 ++++++-----
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

--- a/editors/patch.c
+++ b/editors/patch.c
@@ -177,8 +177,6 @@
 
 static void fail_hunk(void)
 {
-	if (!TT.current_hunk) return;
-
 	fdprintf(2, "Hunk %d FAILED %ld/%ld.\n", TT.hunknum, TT.oldline, TT.newline);
 	TT.exitval = 1;
 
@@ -186,9 +184,12 @@
 	// this file and advance to next file.
 
 	TT.state = 2;
-	TT.current_hunk->prev->next = NULL;
-	dlist_free(TT.current_hunk, do_line);
-	TT.current_hunk = NULL;
+	// (The hunk is empty if its first line was invalid.)
+	if (TT.current_hunk) {
+		TT.current_hunk->prev->next = NULL;
+		dlist_free(TT.current_hunk, do_line);
+		TT.current_hunk = NULL;
+	}
 
 	// Abort the copy and delete the temporary file.
 	close(TT.filein);
